<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SActionScript version="1" name="plugin_restart_no_livelock">

  <!-- A PLUGIN THAT ASKS TO BE RESTARTED MUST NOT STOP PLAYBACK FROM STARTING.

       THE BUG THIS CASE EXISTS FOR, in the order it happens:

         1. the readahead freezes a page out of order, so
            twPluginSlotProcessor resets the plugin instance
            ("[slot] non-sequential page at N; resetting plugin state");
         2. the plugin answers the reset by asking the host to restart it.
            THIS IS NORMAL AND EVERY iPlug2 PLUGIN DOES IT: OnReset() reports
            the plugin's latency, and both IPlugCLAP::SetLatency() and
            IPlugVST3::SetLatency() notify the host UNCONDITIONALLY, without
            comparing the new number with the old one;
         3. SPluginNativeEditor's 30 Hz poll reports the restart and calls
            SPluginSlot::notifyPluginEdited(), which stales the whole render
            path above the slot;
         4. every page is re-frozen, out of order, and we are back at (1).

       The readahead can never reach the three seconds of buffer the transport
       waits for, so PLAYBACK NEVER STARTS. Measured on a real project: the log
       printed "[PLAYBACK] Waiting for readahead to build buffer..." forever,
       and one worker sat in the plugin's process() while the content epoch
       climbed underneath it.

       IT NEEDS THE PLUGIN'S OWN WINDOW OPEN, which is why this case opens one:
       the restart flag has no drain anywhere else (twClapPlugin::poll /
       twVst3Editor::poll are only called by the editor). That is also the
       user-facing workaround, and it is what made the report reproducible.

       WHAT IS ASSERTED — and WHAT IS NOT, which matters here:

         * THE MECHANISM, and it is the whole gate. At most ONE restart is
           acted on across a second of playback, and the guard is SEEN
           declining the rest. Both halves are needed: without the second
           assertion the case would also pass on a build where the plugin
           simply never asked for anything.

         * NOT THE SYMPTOM. `wait-playhead` is here so the case cannot pass
           with the transport standing still, but it DOES NOT reproduce the
           starvation and it is not evidence of one. Measured with the guard
           disabled: about 25 restarts per second, each staling the render
           path — and the playhead still advanced 48128 frames in 1168 ms,
           because a 4-second sawtooth through one arithmetic-only plugin
           re-freezes faster than the loop can stale it. The report this case
           comes from is a guitar-amp plugin at 4x oversampling on several
           tracks, where the freeze is the slow side and the loop wins. A
           fixture that could starve the readahead would have to burn real CPU
           per page, and a gate whose failure mode is "the suite got slower"
           is not one worth having.

       THE FIXTURE IS THE POINT. tw.test.clap.restart is tw.test.clap.gui plus
       one habit — request_restart() from activate() and reset(), always — which
       is iPlug2's behaviour reduced to the one line a host can observe. It
       turns no knob (its GUI twin does; see twtestclap.c), so a parameter edit
       cannot stand in for the thing being measured.

       This gates the FORMAT-NEUTRAL half of the fix, in SPluginNativeEditor:
       a restart that changes neither the reported latency nor the parameter
       count has nothing in it. The VST3 half (twvst3host.h: a restart raised
       from inside a call the host itself made is never reported) is NOT gated
       here and has no fixture — twtestvst3.cpp's createView returns nullptr, so
       there is no headless route to a VST3 editor poll at all. -->

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <insert-plugin trackPath="0" slotIndex="0"
                   format="clap" uid="tw.test.clap.restart"
                   name="Smaragd Test Restart" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="twtestclap.clap" nIn="2" nOut="2"/>

    <!-- The fixture carries clap.gui, so the slot must resolve to the native
         editor: without that there is nothing to open and nothing to poll. -->
    <assert-plugin-editor-kind trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" expect="native"/>

    <!-- 1. Open the window (no window is created — the fixture's gui allocates
         nothing) and let the poll settle. Any restart left over from the
         instance being prepared is consumed HERE, so the window measured below
         starts clean. -->
    <plugin-native-editor trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" action="open" expectOpen="1"/>
    <wait-ms ms="300"/>

    <!-- 2. Play. The readahead freezes pages, the pages provoke resets, and the
         resets provoke restarts — the loop, if there is one. -->
    <set-locator position="0"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>
    <wait-playhead position="48000" minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="30000"/>

    <!-- 3. A second of ordinary playback is the assert-log WINDOW: the window is
         the records since the PREVIOUS action started, and two assert-logs in a
         row share it. -->
    <wait-ms ms="1000"/>

    <!-- Pre-fix this window held one of these per poll — about thirty per
         second, each one staling the render path. -->
    <assert-log contains="native editor: plugin requested a restart"
                minCount="0" maxCount="1"/>

    <!-- ...and this is the guard doing it, rather than the plugin having gone
         quiet. It is a DEBUG line; the ring keeps every level. -->
    <assert-log contains="restart with no observable change" minCount="1"/>

    <toggle-playback play="0"/>

    <!-- An editor still alive when its plugin is destroyed is a contract
         violation the CLAP backend logs as an error. -->
    <plugin-native-editor trackIndex="0" slotIndex="0" action="close" expectOpen="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
